  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nDeep, layered moisture continues over the CWA from the RG Valley \neast with PWs of around an inch. This could be from another upper \ntrough that rotated around the upper low over SoCal. Our round of \nsevere weather seemed to align with the back edge of this layered \nmoisture, with convection now weakened considerably and mostly \nover Otero County. \n\nSaturday and Sunday...another upper trough now over western \nArizona will rotate eastward Saturday, reaching the RG Valley mid \nafternoon. This looks to be the convective initiator, limiting \nmost of the thunderstorms to the valley east starting mid \nafternoon. MUCAPES approach 1000 J/kg in the east Saturday, and \nmuch like today, decent speed shear with little directional shear.\nThus, some severe storms are possible again on Saturday in the \neast. Same story mostly on Sunday, though as the upper low lifts \nout over Utah and the main trough from the low moves into New \nMexico, the convective focus may shift even a bit further east. \nThe environment still keeps MUCAPES of around 500-1000 J/kg, but \nwith mid level winds turning more southwest, some directional \nshear is introduced back into the area. Hail likely to be the \nbiggest threat again; some flooding is possible but lower PWs and \nthe continued rapid movement of the storms should keep this threat\nlower. \n\nMonday through Wednesday...drier air at upper levels moves in as \nupper flow turns westerly. However mid level remains southerly \nMonday so slight chance of storms remains over Hudspeth/Otero \nCounties. Tuesday and Wednesday should remain dry.\n\nThursday and Friday...mid and upper level winds become a bit more\nsouth/southwest as a Pacific trough approaches the west coast. \nThis will draw up modest amounts of moisture, so a slight chance \nof rain will also follow for both these days.",
